    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Latin, the language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latium">Latium</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, spread with Roman power across the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and became the language of law, government and a great literature from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a> onwards.</li><li>Spoken Latin changed region by region into the Romance languages, among them Spanish, Portuguese, French, Italian and Romanian, now the first languages of nearly 900 million people.</li><li>Written Latin outlived the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> by more than a thousand years as the language of the Church, the universities, science and diplomacy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Latin remained the official language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> until 1844, when the Hungarian diet made Hungarian the language of government in its place.</p></aside>
    </section>

